    <string name="about_title">About</string>
    <string name="about_body">Loviedowpie is for you and one partner. There is no account, no analytics, and no ads. Location, battery, photos, and ring commands are encrypted so the relay cannot read them.</string>
    <string name="version_line">Version %1$s</string>

    <string name="privacy_title">Where your data goes</string>
    <string name="privacy_intro">This is the same explanation as PRIVACY-DATA-FLOW.md.</string>
    <string name="privacy_location">Location, battery, your name, your photo, and ring commands leave the phone only as encrypted messages. They go to the relay, which stores ciphertext and cannot read it. Only your partner can.</string>
    <string name="privacy_code">The pairing code is shown on your screen. This app does not send the code. The relay sees a one-way id and the pairing handshake, not the code.</string>
    <string name="privacy_keys">Encryption keys stay on the phone, wrapped by the Android keystore.</string>
    <string name="privacy_fcm">If wake-ups are set up, this phone\'s FCM token is sent to the relay so it can ask Google to ping you. The ping is empty. It has no location and no message.</string>
    <string name="privacy_tiles">When you open the map, tile images for the area on screen are downloaded from the OpenStreetMap tile server. That server sees the area you view and your IP address. It does not receive your partner\'s name, and it is not told that a coordinate belongs to them.</string>
    <string name="privacy_update">Update checks download a public version file and, if you ask, an APK from the relay. They contain no pairing data.</string>
    <string name="privacy_not">There is no analytics, no ads, and no account. The app does not ask for a phone number or an email address.</string>
    <string name="privacy_keep">Relay messages expire on their own (about a day for messages, about 10 minutes for pairing).</string>

